## ArrayList Contract Tests

The `PUT_GetContract` is a method to test the contract of an ArrayList:

```csharp
[PexMethod]
[PexExploration]
public void PUT_GetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int index)
```

1. `arrList` is the ArrayList that we want to test.
2. `index` is the index at which we want to perform a test.

The test generates a count of the ArrayList before and after the operation and checks if the ArrayList contains the value we have just retrieved via the indexer.

```csharp
int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;

int New_Ret = (int)(arrList[index]);

int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
bool New_containsNewRet = arrList.Contains(New_Ret);
``` 

Finally it observes the values using `PexObserve.ValueForViewing` .

The `PUT_GetContract` method is annotated with `[PexMethod]` and `[PexExploration]` attributes, which signifies that this is a potential state transition test method and it's intented to be explored extensively by the Peex tool. 

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[HostType("Moles")]
[TestCategory("BVT")]
public void Put_GetContract_ArrayList()
{
    // Arrange
    ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList();
    arrList.Add(1);
    arrList.Add(2);
    arrList.Add(3);

    // Act & Assert
    // PexExplorationAttribute will discover this and
    // generate tests for all "exploration" methods and their parameters
    PUT_GetContract(arrList, 0);
}
```  

With the test rig defined, you can run exploratory unit tests in your project using the Pex tool, which will perform a variety of tests, such as edge and boundary cases, and generate tests for all "exploration" methods and their parameters.

The test rig generates not only the usual Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) setup, but it also generates tests for the contract of both the get and add operations. The get operation being tested is reading an element from an ArrayList given a particular index, and the add operation is adding an element at a specific index in the ArrayList.

The test rig can now be used to generate tests for the get operation (also known as indexer contract tests), which in turn can be used to verify the get operation behaves as specified in the IList<T>.this[int index] definition for the ArrayList class.

## References

1. [Unit Testing Pitfalls](http://blog.jetbrains.com/dotnet/2009/09/22/unit-testing-pitfalls/)
2. [MSDN ArrayList](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.collections.arraylist?view=net-5.0)
3. [MSTest](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/unit-test-basics?view=vs-2019)
4. [Pex: Test Method Generation](https://github.com/microsoft/testfx-docs/blob/master/Docs/UserGuide.md) 
5. [ArrayList Contract Testing](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/unit-test-basics?view=vs-2019) - A step by step guide
6. [Code Contracts in C#](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/framework/performance/contracts/) */---
