``` 

The PexAssumeUnderTest attribute denotes the target method that the Pex engine should execute.

The `AssumePrecondition.IsTrue` method indicates that is certain that the index is within the range of the input array.

The `PexObserve.ValueForViewing` is used to display the values that you would like to see when the test runs. In this case, we have displayed the original array count, the new array count, old index, the value returned, and whether the new value is in the array.

The final assert is placed in the "@@@@ assert" box for analysis by the Pex engine. Here it verifies that the new array contains the value returned.

Remember that Pex is a visual tool and its behavior might change with different versions of Visual Studio. Furthermore, this example assumes a little about how the ArrayList class works and the inputs/outputs of its methods which might not be accurate and you should use it as a guideline rather than a solid code verification tool.

Also, please note Pex runs the code under several possible execution paths and verifies that each one behaves correctly. So while it can help to generate reasonable test data, it does not replace a good understanding of the code or a careful design of your tests.

