# <a name="how-to-run-test-scenarios"></a>Como executar cenários de teste

Você pode usar a funcionalidade de execução de cenários de teste no Visual Studio para automatizar seus testes, executar testes de carga e executar testes no Microsoft Azure.

Esses recursos avançados são necessários para certos cenários de teste, mas também podem adicionar complexidade extra a seu ambiente de teste. Se você estiver executando testes de carga, por exemplo, é melhor executá-los remotamente para evitar afetar o sistema sob teste. Isso ainda permite obter as estatísticas do teste de alta fidelidade sem afetar seus sistemas críticos.

## <a name="run-scenario-tests"></a>Executar testes de cenário

1. No Visual Studio, abra o menu de atalho no projeto de teste que você deseja executar clicando na Gaveta de Testes na parte superior da tela e escolhendo **Definir Fila de Teste**.

2. Na caixa de diálogo **Gerenciador de Configuraç�es de Teste**, escolha o conjunto de testes que deseja executar e clique em **OK**. Se você não vir a Gaveta de Testes, escolha **Exibir > Gaveta de Testes--------------------	    Debug.Assert((New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount || New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount - 1) , "Array does not contain New_Ret .");
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ the array does not contain Old_index 
	    Debug.Assert((New_containsNewRet | !arrList.Contains(Old_index)) , "Array contains Old_index .");
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ the array does not contain New_Ret 
	    Debug.Assert((arrList[index] == New_Ret), "Array at index contains incorrect value .");
        }

        public void PUT_GetContract()
        {
            int index;
            ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList { 1, 2, 3 };

            PUT_GetContract(arrList, index);
        }
    }
}
```
 
When running the test above, you may get a Pass/Fail result or an error. Here is the explanation:
 
- The PutGetContract method simulates a call to `arrList[index]` and expects to retrieve the same value. In this test, we assume that the index is within the valid range of the array list, otherwise the test will be skipped.
- The `index` is assumed to be randomly selected.
- After calling the PutGetContract method, we observe the old array list count, the new array list count, the old index, the retrieved value, and whether the new list contains the retrieved value.
- We then perform assertions on these observations.

Remember, these are just the assertions included in the test. Depending on the specific circumstances of the code or the requirements of the project, you might need to add more assertions.

### Conclusion: ###
To conclude, the PexMethod attribute is a powerful tool that increases the coverage of our tests and prevents regression bugs. It allows us to automatically generate a wide variety of test conditions that test our code against different possible inputs. This kind of testing is efficient and helps us in quickly identifying any potential problems in our code or its interactions with other systems. However, a significant downside of Pex is that it has a steep learning curve. While it provides high coverage for standard programming scenarios, users must adjust the settings and tweak their test cases to ensure the highest possible effectiveness, depending on the specific needs of the project or system under test. Furthermore, the tool is not suited for every type of problem, but rather excels at generating test coverage for complex scenarios involving mutable data and interactions between multiple components. Furthermore, the tool doesn’t support testing code written in languages not directly supported by the tool. Overall, while Pex can be a potent tool for testing purposes, its effectiveness strongly depends on the specific code scenario and test needs.
 
>“One of the best tools we could ever use, but the best tool does not always win a beauty competition.”
>– Oscar Wilde 

