```
- In the example provided, PexAssumeUnderTest attribute is used to indicate that the first field of the method is the target to be tested under.
- A precondition is assumed to be true for the second field: index < arrList.Count && index >= 0.
- The method under test should return the corresponding value at the specified index in the array list.
- The method will return a value for the specified index, so the initial precondition is true in this case.
- After removing an element, the size of the list should be reduced by 1;
- The list after the removal should contain the old value at the old index;
- The size of the list should still be the same after the removal;
- It should be true that the list contains the retrieved value;

It's worth noting that when testing through Partition, Pex only runs a small subset of possible parameter values for the method under test when Pex is run in Partitioning mode. This ensures that tests run faster, but may result in missing potential bug patterns.

So Pex run can find all bugs when it is run in the Verification mode. It’s also important to remember that Pex does not guarantee 100% bug detection for every single code branch and data combination that it checks.

P.S: Pex can't be used for all situations e.g. performance testing, or testing multi-threaded or I/O-based code.

```
[TestClass]
[TestCategory("ArrayList")]
public class ArrayListVerificationTests
{
    [TestMethod]
    public void Pex_Verification_ArrayList()
    {
        var pexSettings = new PexSettings
        {
            MaxConstraintSolverTime = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(60),
            MaxStates = 1000
        };
        
        var testTarget = new ArrayList();
        int index = pexSettings.GenerateInputFor<int>();
        testTarget.Add(index);

        try
        {
            var result = testTarget[index];
            Assert.IsTrue(testTarget.Contains(result));
        }
        catch (System.Exception e)
        {
            var stackTrace = e.StackTrace;
            Assert.IsTrue("The index is out of bounds." == e.Message);
        }
    }
}
```

This is a simple unit test that uses Pex to verify the correctness of ArrayList's indexer. To provide the inputs for the indexer, Pex generates a random number - in this case, for the index of the list - and it's then added to the list. It then tries to access the list at this index and checks two things:
- it doesn't throw out of bounds exception
- the result is contained in the list it was added to.

Pex will generate various test inputs such as negative and positive numbers, 0 and over the size of the list, etc.
```python
// A sample usage of Pex in C# test
public void PexTest(ArrayList arrList)
{
    int index = PexSettings.GenerateInputFor<int>(); // Let Pex generate a random value
    Assert.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0); // Precondition

    // Our method to be tested:
    int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    arrList.Add(index);
    int Old_index = index;
    int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    bool New_containsOldRet = arrList.Contains(Old_index);

    // Assertions:
    Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount + 1, New_arrListCount);
    Assert.IsTrue(New_containsOldRet);
}
```python
