    // Our method to be tested:
    int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    arrList.Add(index);
    int Old_index = index;
    int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    bool New_--------------------            Assert.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(New_Ret));
        }
    }
}

[TestClass]
public class ArrayListContractTest
{
    [TestMethod]
    [PexMethod]
    [PexExpectedException(typeof(System.ArgumentOutOfRangeException), "index")]
    public void PUT_GetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int index)
    {
        new ArrayListContractTest.ArrayList_ArrayListContractTest().PUT_GetContract(arrList, index);
    }
}
```

The PexObserve.ValueForViewing statements are optional and are used to make the results of the generated tests visible in the Pex Visualizer. The test asserts that after invoking the Get method on the ArrayList instance, the value returned is present within the ArrayList. The [PexExpectedException] attribute is used to specify the type of exception that the Pex engine is expected to throw.
```html
ArrayListContractTest.cs has been saved.
```

```csharp
// ArrayListContains.cs
using System;
using System.Collections;

namespace PexDemo
{
    class ArrayListContains
    {
        static void Main()
        {
            // Create an ArrayList.
            ArrayList arrayList = new ArrayList();
            
            // Add elements to the ArrayList.
            arrayList.Add(5);
            arrayList.Add('a');
            arrayList.Add(2.3);

            // Display the ArrayList.
            Console.WriteLine("ArrayList contains:");
            foreach (var item in arrayList)
            {
                Console.WriteLine(item);
            }
            Console.ReadLine();

            // Test the Contains method.
            Console.WriteLine("Searching for 'a' in ArrayList...");
            if (arrayList.Contains('a'))
            {
                Console.WriteLine("Element 'a' found in ArrayList!");
            }
            else
            {
                Console.WriteLine("Element 'a' not found in ArrayList!");
            }

            // Attempt to search for a non-existing element.
            Console.WriteLine("Searching for 'z' in ArrayList...");
            if (arrayList.Contains('z'))
            {
                Console.WriteLine("Element 'z' found in ArrayList!");
            }
            else
            {
                Console.WriteLine("Element 'z' not found in ArrayList!");
            }

            Console.ReadLine();
        }
    }
}
```

The Console output will be:
```
ArrayList contains:
5
a
2.3

Searching for 'a' in ArrayList...
Element 'a' found in ArrayList!

Searching for 'z' in ArrayList...
Element 'z' not found in ArrayList!
```

In the Main method, an ArrayList is created and elements are added to it. The Contains method is then tested, searching for 'a' (which exists in the ArrayList) and 'z' (which does not exist in the ArrayList). During the runtime, the ArrayList's state is inspected using a foreach loop, printing each element to the console.--------------------	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount 
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ old_arrList_index == Index 
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ old_arrList[old_arrList_index] == Old_arrListCount 
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ old_arrList contains New_Ret 
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ new_arrList_Count == Old_arrListCount 
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ new_arrList_containsNewRet 
        }
    }
}

{% endhighlight %}</div>

As before, the main part of the contract test is the PUT_Get contract. However, this time, we also have additional assertions after each step of the test. The goal here is to confirm that all the aspects of the GET operation (retrieving an item at a specific index) are behaving correctly. This includes that the length of the list doesn't change, that the index used to retrieve the item is still valid, and that the value retrieved matches the one in the old array. Furthermore, the new content of the array includes the value just retrieved, and the length of the array hasn't changed.

In the real-world implementation of contract testing in testing method contracts, you will normally have more detailed observations and more precise assertions. And you still may need to use other types of assertions according to the actual problem at hand.{% assign img=site.url | append:"/assets/images/" | append:page.img %}  <img src="{{ img }}" alt="{{ page.title }}" title="{{ page.title }}"/>

Notice that the contract of the ArrayList method `[GET] GetItem(int index)` is expressed with conditions on the states of collections before and after the operation.

Remember to check all potential edge cases when writing your contract to make sure your assertions are robust. Edge cases might be items at the beginning or end of the array, items in the middle, index 0, or negative index, getting items past the end of the array and so on. If your array list can have duplicate items, then ensure that your contract checks for that as well. 

Remember also that it is good practice to test your contracts under different assumptions (with different inputs) to ensure that your contract is always correct and that it behaves correctly regardless of the input it receives.

You may also want to extend your contract testing to other mutator methods such `SetItem` and `AddItem` for further verify their reliability. Also consider concurrent test scenarios by using `PexThreadStaticAttribute` and `PexDataStoreLocationAttribute` attributes to ensure that your contract behaves consistently across different threads or by different callers. 

This completes the list of the techniques you should use for testing method contracts, making sure that your code behaves as expected under various conditions under different assumptions of input. This will cover a vast range of potential edge cases and make your test suite more robust and reliable.

Please, keep in mind that testing method contracts might require certain environment configurations too. Code contracts need to be manually enabled in a test environment before they can be utilized. Debug mode must be enabled for code contracts to provide accurate data and exception information. Moreover, static code analysis feature in Visual Studio must be turned on to check contracts. Also, the 'Contract Program Checker' by Microsoft Research must be installed to execute and analyze code contracts.

You should always have a sound and thorough understanding of your test suite and your code base before and during manual or automated testing and debugging sessions. This will allow you to write more effective and efficient methods contracts, and can help you catch bugs earlier in the development process. If it happens that your contract does not produce the expected results, you'll be able to trace them back to the exact location of the bug in your code. This can be extremely helpful for both the developers and the testers involved in the development process.

Please, feel free to comment or ask questions about this or other topics, I'm here to listen and help.

{% endhighlight %}

